  "stemBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "The points P and Q have coordinates P(−4, 1) and Q(2, 9). (a) Write the vector PQ as a column vector. (b) Calculate |PQ|. (c) Write a unit vector in the direction from P to Q."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Think first: The vector from P to Q is always the destination minus the starting point."
    },
    {
      "type": "asset",
      "storagePath": "assets/question_visuals/p181_q07.jpg",
      "altText": "Source visual for edmar-csec-p181-q07 from workbook page 181."
    }
  ],
  "solutionSteps": [
    {
      "stepNo": 1,
      "instruction": "Subtract the coordinates in the right order.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "PQ = Q − P = (2 − (−4), 9 − 1)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: PQ = (6, 8)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 2,
      "instruction": "Apply Pythagoras to the components.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "|PQ|² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: |PQ| = 10"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 3,
      "instruction": "Divide the vector by its magnitude.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "(1/10) × (6, 8)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: unit vector = (0.6, 0.8)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stepNo": 4,
      "instruction": "Check the unit vector.",
      "contentBlocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "0.6² + 0.8² = 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Result: direction preserved, length 1"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "strategyBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Subtract P's coordinates from Q's, use Pythagoras for the magnitude, then divide the vector by that magnitude."
    }
  ],
  "finalAnswerBlocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "(a) PQ = (6, 8)  (b) |PQ| = 10  (c) unit vector = (1/10)(6, 8) = (0.6, 0.8), that is (3/5, 4/5)"
    }
  ],
  "whyThisWorks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "Going from P to Q means undoing P's position and then applying Q's, which is exactly Q − P. The magnitude is the straight-line distance between the points, so this is the distance formula in vector clothing. Dividing by that distance rescales the arrow to length 1 while leaving its direction untouched, which is what makes a unit vector the standard way to record 'this way' with no size attached."
    }
  ],
